Note the loop structure differs from level3.c: level3.c calls KERNEL(min_i, min_jj, min_l) once per (row-block, col-chunk); the kernel internally splits rows into unroll_M chunks and columns into unroll_N chunks. np_gemm_i splits via lk_row_chunks and lk_col_chunks first (based on unroll sizes) then computes each element with np_kacc modeling the kernel's internal accumulation. Also k-loop order: level3.c does js → ls → is → jjs (n-block outer, k-block middle, rows, columns). The repo does ls → calls → col chunks → row chunks → i → j. Since each element c[j*n+i] += acc per k-block, and within a k-block, each element is computed by the kernel with its own lane pattern — the sum ORDER within one (row, col) element depends only on the kernel's lane pattern for that chunk size (rs, cs) — not on loop nesting order across elements. So it's equivalent. 

Also, importantly, the repo's np_kacc models dgemm_kernel_16x2_skylakex.c: for cs==12 with rs==4: w=2 lanes; rs 2/1: w=4; else w=1 chain. This models the "first" calls (packed B columns) — wait no, wok = !first. For first=true, wok=false → plain chain (w=1). For subsequent, wok=true and cs==12? Hmm, cs is the column chunk size: for skx dgemm UNROLL_N=2, jjsz gives 6*2=12 columns chunks, so cs=12 for full chunks. The kernel's internal 16x2: rows processed 4 at a time (4x12 panels?) with... this was all empirically found for dgemm.

